module stdlib.env

# The process environment. Declared here rather than in the prelude: nothing in the prelude calls
# it, and under the rule "an extern lives in the module that owns its surface" this is that module.
#
# Why not stdlib.process, where the environment arguably belongs — it is, after all, the state
# every child inherits? Because both were measured. stdlib.process carries the whole subprocess
# API, and there is no cross-module dead-code elimination (BACKLOG §7 "no cross-module DCE"), so
# importing it to read one config switch pulls in twelve unrelated definitions: proc_run,
# ProcResult, and the Bytes→String decode chain. examples/io_do_demo grew by 247 lines of IR that
# way, against ~12 through this module, and the compiler seed picked up eleven
# `@stdlib.process.*` bodies because infer.sprout reads one SPROUT_TRACE_DISPATCH switch. A
# refactor whose whole point is that programs stop carrying builtins they do not use cannot pay
# that. So: a leaf module, no imports, matching stdlib.fs and stdlib.time.
#
# `export` on an `extern fn` would be inert: the parser discards it (parser.sprout:1941) and the
# bundler never registers extern names (bundler.sprout:597), so an extern is reachable by bare name
# from anywhere this module is bundled. The wrapper below is what makes the reach intentional and
# greppable, not a visibility rule. It also cannot be named `env_get` — that would collide with the
# extern it wraps.
#
# This module once carried a "NO TYPE DECLARATIONS, deliberately" rule, cross-referencing the same
# note in stdlib/fs.sprout: a `type` would have dropped the module out of the REPL, whose loader
# validated type-decl field names against a fixed vocabulary and swallowed the failure. That
# constraint is GONE — Fix A of docs/repl-env-type-vocabulary-v0.md (`@type:` env markers) retired
# it, and stdlib.fs now declares types and still loads. Declare a type here if one is warranted.
extern fn env_get(name: String) -> Maybe String !{IO}

# `Nothing` means the name is unset. A name set to the EMPTY STRING is `Just ""`, not `Nothing`:
# the C only returns `Nothing` when `getenv` gives NULL (sprout_runtime.c:2880), and POSIX
# distinguishes an empty value from an absent one. Code testing for "configured" should therefore
# match the constructor, not the emptiness of the value.
export fn get(name: String) -> Maybe String !{IO} =
  env_get(name)
